{
  "configuration_id": "config_025",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best is config_019 (multimodal, multiprofile, PCA, ResNet50, avgpool, weighted; acc=0.639). Key findings: (1) multimodal >> RNA-only, (2) multiprofile >> singleprofile (~9% gain), (3) avgpool > transformer for aggregation, (4) weighted > concat for fusion. The one major design axis untested within the winning configuration is the image encoder: we have only used ResNet50, while ImageNet ViT-L is available and substantially more expressive. Question: Does upgrading the image encoder from ResNet50 to ViT-L further improve the already-strong multimodal pipeline, or is the image contribution already saturated? Why this experiment: config_025 is the minimal, direct extension of the current best (only swapping the image encoder), so any performance change is cleanly attributable to encoder capacity. Given that the image modality contributes meaningfully (multimodal vs. RNA-only gap), resolving whether a stronger visual backbone helps is the highest-information next step under a limited budget, and it avoids confounding multiple changes."
}

